💡 What is a WebSocket?

WebSocket is a protocol that allows a persistent, two-way (full-duplex) communication channel between a client (like a web browser)
and a server — over a single TCP connection.

In simple terms:
➡️ Unlike HTTP, which is request–response-based (client always initiates),
➡️ WebSocket lets both client and server send messages anytime after the connection is established.

🔄 How it works (in short)

Client sends a WebSocket handshake request to the server (over HTTP).

Server responds and upgrades the connection from HTTP → WebSocket protocol.

After that, both sides can send data anytime without reconnecting again.

⚙️ Example
Traditional HTTP:
Client → Server: "Give me latest messages"
Server → Client: "Here they are"
(Client must ask again and again)

WebSocket:
Client ↔ Server (open connection)
Server → Client: "New message received"
Client → Server: "Message sent"
(Both communicate instantly)

🚀 Why We Use WebSockets
| Reason                      | Explanation                                                   |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Real-time communication** | No need to refresh or poll repeatedly — updates are instant.  |
| **Efficiency**              | Less overhead than HTTP — no repeated headers or connections. |
| **Bi-directional**          | Both client and server can send messages anytime.             |
| **Low latency**             | Great for apps where delay must be minimal.                   |

🔧 Common Use Cases
| Use Case                | Example                             |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| **Chat applications**   | WhatsApp Web, Slack, Discord        |
| **Live dashboards**     | Live Stock prices, analytics panels      |
| **Online gaming**       | Multiplayer coordination            |
| **Live notifications**  | Push updates, alerts                |
| **Collaborative tools** | Google Docs-style real-time editing |

🧠 Example in Java (Server Side)

Using Spring Boot:

@ServerEndpoint("/chat")
public class ChatSocket {

    @OnOpen
    public void onOpen(Session session) {
        System.out.println("Connected: " + session.getId());
    }

    @OnMessage
    public void onMessage(String message, Session session) {
        System.out.println("Message: " + message);
        session.getBasicRemote().sendText("Server received: " + message);
    }

    @OnClose
    public void onClose(Session session) {
        System.out.println("Closed: " + session.getId());
    }
}


And the client (browser) connects like:

const socket = new WebSocket("ws://localhost:8080/chat");
socket.onmessage = (event) => console.log("Received:", event.data);
socket.send("Hello Server!");

🧾 Summary
| Feature       | WebSocket      | HTTP                  |
| ------------- | -------------- | --------------------- |
| Connection    | Persistent     | Reconnects each time  |
| Communication | Bi-directional | Request–response only |
| Latency       | Very low       | Higher                |
| Use case      | Real-time apps | Traditional REST APIs |

